.TH LEGION-POWERCTL 8 "2026-08-07" "legion-powerctl 0.3.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
legion-powerctl \- configure Ryzen CPU power limits on Lenovo Legion laptops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B legion-powerctl
.RI COMMAND " [arguments]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B legion-powerctl
validates named profiles, applies RyzenAdj package-power and temperature limits,
and optionally controls powerprofilesctl, CPUFreq boundaries, boost, and AMD
P-State EPP. Its systemd service is a one-shot unit that runs once at boot.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B apply [PROFILE] [--dry-run] [--boot]
Apply a named profile or the selected active profile. A powerbench-* scratch
profile is refused; legion-powerbench applies it through configure --apply. If
config.conf names a scratch profile or sets no ACTIVE_PROFILE, balanced is
applied instead and the command exits with an error naming the reason.
.TP
.B configure PROFILE [OPTIONS]
Create or update a profile. Options include --stapm, --slow, --fast, --temp,
--power-profile, --min-mhz, --max-mhz, --boost, --epp, --description,
--select, and --apply. With --select and --apply, the boot profile changes only
after the apply succeeds.
.TP
.B wizard [PROFILE]
Open an interactive profile editor. powerbench-* scratch names are refused.
.TP
.B select PROFILE [--apply]
Select the profile used at boot. The profile is validated first. With --apply,
config.conf is written only after the apply succeeds, and only if the profile
still exists then. powerbench-* scratch profiles cannot be selected.
.TP
.B list
List profiles. The active profile is marked with an asterisk.
.TP
.B show [PROFILE]
Show a profile, defaulting to the active profile.
.TP
.B delete PROFILE [--force]
Delete a profile. The active profile is protected unless --force is used.
Forcing the delete of the boot profile moves boot to balanced, and is refused
when balanced is missing or invalid, or is itself the boot profile. Delete refuses while another power operation holds
the lock.
.TP
.B status [--json|--waybar]
Show configuration, service state, CPU driver, frequency range, and the most
recent apply record, including partial failures. With --json, print a single-line
machine-readable document carrying a schema_version field (currently 1) plus
every profile; the GUI and widgets consume this. With --waybar, print the JSON
object a Waybar custom module expects (text, alt, class, tooltip).
.TP
.B doctor
Run compatibility and installation checks, including RyzenAdj version,
Secure Boot/kernel-lockdown state, and conflicting power-management tools.
.TP
.B repair balanced-plus [--dry-run]
Back up the existing profile and module configuration, then apply the shipped
balanced-plus values of 70/75/85 W at 72 C, balanced mode, stock CPU frequencies, boost on and
balance_performance EPP. Save the repaired profile only after the apply succeeds.
If a compatible but incomplete ryzen_smu module blocks RyzenAdj, unload it without
force when lockdown is none and /dev/mem exists. After a successful apply, blacklist
its automatic loading and remove only the tool-owned modules-load entry.
An initramfs containing the module may need rebuilding before reboot.
The dry run prints the planned changes without writing files or unloading modules.
.TP
.B enable [--force]
Run doctor, then enable the boot service and apply the selected profile.
Refuses when doctor reports failures unless --force is supplied.
.TP
.B disable
Disable the boot service. Existing hardware state remains until firmware or a
reboot changes it.
.TP
.B restore-frequency [--boost on|off|unchanged] [--dry-run]
Restore the hardware-reported CPUFreq minimum and maximum, and update the
frequency and boost fields of the last-apply record to match.
Use --dry-run to preview the writes without changing controls.
.TP
.B baseline [--capture|--show]
Record the firmware's own SMU limits, or print the recorded ones; the two flags
cannot be combined. The capture stores nothing unless all four limits read back as
numbers, and a complete capture is never overwritten. It is refused once any apply or
repair has started to write limits this boot, because the SMU no longer holds the
firmware values by then. The first apply after installation captures them
automatically and, if it could not, says so once on standard output rather than as
a warning.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LEGION_POWERCTL_DRY_RUN=1
Make apply, repair and restore-frequency preview without changing anything.
Every other mutating subcommand refuses to run while it is set.
.TP
.B LEGION_POWERCTL_TESTING
and the other LEGION_POWERCTL_* variables exist for the test suite. A process
running as root ignores all of them except LEGION_POWERCTL_DRY_RUN.
.PP
RyzenAdj is never looked up on PATH: only /usr/local/bin/ryzenadj and
/usr/bin/ryzenadj are used, and only when owned by root and not writable by
group or others, like every directory above them. As root, powerprofilesctl and systemctl are not looked up on
PATH either: they come from /usr/local/sbin, /usr/local/bin, /usr/sbin, /usr/bin,
/sbin or /bin, under the same ownership check.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success and non-zero on failure. The command never exits 126 or 127 itself: a child that does is reported as 1, because pkexec
uses those two codes for a dismissed or refused authorization.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/legion-powerctl/config.conf
Selected active profile.
.TP
.I /etc/legion-powerctl/profiles.d/*.conf
Profile definitions.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/legion-powerctl.service
One-shot boot service.
.TP
.I /run/legion-powerctl/last-apply.env
Volatile record of the last apply operation, whether it completed, and whether its
limits read back: VERIFIED=yes, partial, no or unknown.
.TP
.I /run/legion-powerctl/smu-written
The boot ID of a boot in which an apply or repair started to write SMU limits.
.TP
.I /var/lib/legion-powerctl/backups/repair-*
Original profile and module configuration preserved by repair.
.TP
.I /etc/modprobe.d/legion-powerctl-no-ryzen-smu.conf
Recovery blacklist for an incomplete optional SMU module.
.TP
.I /var/lib/legion-powerctl/stock-limits.env
The firmware SMU limits, recorded once before the first apply overwrote them.
.SH GRAPHICAL INTERFACE
.B legion-powerctl-gui
opens an unprivileged Qt control panel (requires the optional pyside6
package). It reads state through 'status --json' and performs privileged
actions through pkexec. The GUI runs seven subcommands: configure, select,
apply (Go back), delete, enable, disable and repair. The shipped policy defines
one action for each (io.github.alexmacra.legion-powerctl.configure, .apply,
.select, .delete, .enable, .disable, .repair), each pinned with exec.argv1.
Editing, applying, and selecting profiles use auth_admin_keep, which polkit
retains for a few minutes; treat those three as one trust tier, because
.B configure
accepts --select and --apply and so reaches what the other two do. Deleting a
profile, enabling or disabling the boot service, and repair always prompt, so a
retained profile-editing authorization cannot be reused to remove a profile,
to install boot persistence, or to unload a module and rewrite a profile.
.SH NOTES
This project is not affiliated with Lenovo, AMD, or CachyOS. Legion is a
trademark of Lenovo. Fire Range and other HX-series CPUs require RyzenAdj
0.19.0 or newer.
.PP
RyzenAdj state is volatile. Boot persistence means reapplying the selected
profile once during startup; it does not mean writing permanent firmware
settings. Firmware profile changes and suspend/resume may overwrite limits on
some laptops.
.PP
On newer Ryzen families, RyzenAdj may successfully apply settings even when
its information or power-metric table is unavailable.
.SH SAFETY
Profile validation prevents malformed input but does not establish that a
specific wattage or temperature is appropriate for every CPU or laptop.
Monitor temperatures and test workloads incrementally.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR powerprofilesctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
